es. Opus 5.5 was ahead for most of the middle game, and after the 12k's F4 on move 27 the lead stood at 9.4 points for White. A run of costly moves followed: H3, H5 and especially G1 on move 36, which lost 8.0 points and swung the lead to Black at +5.7. The 12k never fell behind again after that.", "moments": [{"turn": 20, "text": "Opus 5.5 played B3 and lost 3.3 points, taking the lead from -3.3 to -0.1. The engine preferred B4, and Black took B4 on the next move, so the earlier White edge was gone for now."}, {"turn": 27, "text": "The 12k's F4 pushed along the fourth line and lost 5.6 points, the worst move of the game for Black. It moved the lead from -3.7 to -9.4, which was White's biggest edge of the game. The engine's choice was B1."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with H3 and lost 4.9 points, so a lead of 9.4 shrank to 4.5. The engine's first choice here was F8, and the position stayed tense on the right side."}, {"turn": 32, "text": "H5 lost 6.1 points, a blunder, and the lead went from -6.0 to +0.1, so Black was level with White for the first time since the early middle game. The engine wanted A4 instead."}, {"turn": 36, "text": "G1 was the biggest loss of the game at 8.0 points, moving the lead from -2.3 to +5.7. The engine's choice was A4. Opus 5.5 never got back in front, and Black held the lead until the end."}, {"turn": 42, "text": "J6 was the engine's own choice and gained 0.7 points, a rare clean move in the late fight on the right edge. It was too late to change the result, but it showed Opus 5.5 reading the corner correctly."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py places one move the player names, removes captures, and prints group liberties; libs.py prints liberties for the current board; count.py counts stones and enclosed territory. None of them loops over empty points to choose moves or plays out continuations, so they only inspect the position and the effect of a single named move.", "stop": false}}